From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: OpenMANET Pi 5 port <noreply@openmanet.org>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 21:15:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: bcm2712: Add morse HAT gpios to gpio-line-names

Raspberry Pi 5 counterpart of 991-0005. Same Seeed WM1302 Pi HAT wiring as
the shipping Pi 4 product - reset GPIO17, wake GPIO23, busy GPIO24 - because
it is the same HAT on the same 40-pin header.

The Morse userspace locates the chip reset line by gpio-line-name, not by
number: /morse/scripts/chipreset.sh runs "gpioinfo -s --by-name MM_RESET"
and aborts the reset when the name is absent, and morse_cli.sh greps
/sys/kernel/debug/gpio for MM_RESET. Without this patch neither works on a
Pi 5.

On BCM2712 the 40-pin header GPIOs belong to RP1, not the SoC, so the block
to patch is the one under &rp1_gpio in the arm64 board DTS rather than the
&gpio block in the arm one. RP1 registers a single 54-line gpiochip with
"gpio-ranges = <&rp1_gpio 0 0 54>" (rp1.dtsi) and .base = -1 / .ngpio = 54
(pinctrl-rp1.c), so the line index equals the BCM GPIO number exactly as it
does on the Pi 4 and the three renames transplant positionally. The trailing
"// GPIOn" comments are RP1 style and are kept so the hunk is unambiguous.

bcm2712d0-rpi-5-b.dts needs no change: it #includes this file and overrides
only &gio and &gio_aon, never &rp1_gpio.

Must apply after 950-1180 (which moves the file to arch/arm64) and after
950-0870 (which supplies the "GPIOn" names this hunk uses as context);
the 991-xxxx numbering satisfies both.

---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/broadcom/bcm2712-rpi-5-b.dts | 6 +++---
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/broadcom/bcm2712-rpi-5-b.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/broadcom/bcm2712-rpi-5-b.dts
@@ -699,11 +699,11 @@
 		"GPIO15", // GPIO15
 		"GPIO16", // GPIO16
-		"GPIO17", // GPIO17
+		"MM_RESET", // GPIO17
 		"GPIO18", // GPIO18
 		"GPIO19", // GPIO19
 		"GPIO20", // GPIO20
 		"GPIO21", // GPIO21
 		"GPIO22", // GPIO22
-		"GPIO23", // GPIO23
-		"GPIO24", // GPIO24
+		"MM_WAKE", // GPIO23
+		"MM_BUSY", // GPIO24
 		"GPIO25", // GPIO25
